<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">The Whole Framework &middot; one sitting &middot; about twenty minutes</p>
  <h1>The Summary</h1>
  <p class="lede muted"><strong>I</strong>f you read one page of this site, read this one.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <h2 id="t-sum-thesis">The Thesis</h2>
    <p class="lede">The universe is an eternal integration of opposing infinities, which together swing
      like an out-of-control pendulum.</p>
    <p>Four words there are technical and none of them is decoration. <strong>Integration</strong> is the
      operation from analysis: the gathering of infinitesimal contributions into a total.
      <strong>Opposing infinities</strong> are two that point in different directions and are not the same
      size &mdash; the countable one that opens upward through counting, and the uncountable one that
      opens downward into the interval between zero and one. <strong>Eternal</strong> means the sum does
      not converge, which is arithmetic rather than sentiment. And the <strong>pendulum</strong> is the
      best-studied nonlinear system in physics, whose critical orbit takes infinite time to arrive.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>Everything below is an elaboration of that sentence, and everything on the site is an elaboration
      of what is below. <a href="/foundations/#t-eternal-integration">The long version is in the
      foundations.</a></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading throughout: <span class="sci">&#9670; established science</span> &middot;
      <span class="hist">&#9670; scholarship and history</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; the framework's argument</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">&#10022;</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-sum-chain">The Chain, in Six Links</h2>
    <p>The framework's opening argument is a chain in which each link is forced by the one before. It is
      the thing to attack if you want to attack the whole structure &mdash; break any link and what
      follows is loose.</p>
    <ol class="sum-chain">
      <li><strong>Zero and one are not two quantities but two infinities.</strong> Zero is the floor no
        shrinking quantity reaches; one is the threshold of this rather than that.</li>
      <li><strong>They are unequal.</strong> The interval between them is uncountable; the march upward is
        countable. Cantor's diagonal argument, and not a matter of taste.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>A straight path between them never closes.</strong> To be a relation rather than a gap,
        the path must curve.</li>
      <li><strong>The constant of curving is π.</strong> So π is read as the condition of there being a
        universe, not a number found inside one already running.
        <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>A closed path that returns without repeating is a torus.</strong> The circle repeats
        exactly and so cannot carry a history; the line never returns.</li>
      <li><strong>A change of side without a crossing is a half-twist.</strong> One Möbius turn, and
        inside becomes outside with no boundary passed.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <h2 id="t-sum-chapters">Every Chapter in a Paragraph</h2>

    <h3 id="t-sum-self"><a href="/the-self/">The Self</a></h3>
    <p>A self is an operation, not a thing. A moment of experience is an infinitesimal step and a life is
      the integral of them, which means there is no last term and no arrival. The restlessness that
      follows is the form of the operation rather than a defect in the person.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>

    <h3 id="t-sum-dx"><a href="/dx/">dx</a></h3>
    <p>Mathematics has five working accounts of what a differential is, disagreeing about whether the
      object exists at all &mdash; Weierstrass's notation, Robinson's hyperreals, Lawvere's nilsquares,
      measure theory's d&mu;, and the one-form of exterior calculus, which is not small in the slightest.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> Across all of them d&sup2; = 0 holds, which geometrically says a
      boundary has no boundary. The framework adds a reading: a differential is a completed act of
      gathering, not a quantity caught mid-vanishing, and the grain a mind integrates at is set by
      thermodynamics rather than chosen. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>

    <h3 id="t-sum-splice"><a href="/skeletal-splice/">The Skeletal Splice</a></h3>
    <p>Accumulation is the mechanism, and at scale it stops being invisible. The arithmetic is exact:
      R&#8345; = F&#8345;&#8330;&#8322; &minus; 1, with the deposit per density approaching
      φ&sup2;/&radic;5 = 1.17082&hellip; As temperature is nothing one molecule has and pressure nothing
      one impact has, deposits below the threshold of one accumulate into a measurable gradient, and at
      density they give a new state rather than more of the same. The debt is stated: the thermodynamic
      cases are <em>derived</em>, and the framework has no partition function for thought.</p>

    <h3 id="t-sum-spiral"><a href="/spiral/">The Spiral</a> and <a href="/megatubule/">The Megatubule</a></h3>
    <p>The integers placed on a log spiral land on three fixed points &mdash; minus one, zero, one &mdash;
      giving 0, 333, 666 and 999, with 666 the thirty-sixth triangular number and thirty-six the quark
      count of six flavours by three colours by two. The megatubule takes the half-twist's single forced
      throat and looks for it in the sky; the CMB Cold Spot in Eridanus, about seventy microkelvin cool
      and still unexplained after Szapudi's supervoid and Mackenzie's rebuttal, is an invitation and not a
      confirmation.</p>

    <h3 id="t-sum-torus"><a href="/torus/">The Torus</a> and <a href="/moebius/">The Möbius Strip</a></h3>
    <p>Return without repetition requires a loop around a loop. This is the chapter's strongest card and
      it is not a metaphor: the phase space of a pendulum <em>is</em> a cylinder, because θ and θ + 2π are
      the same configuration, and a closed energy surface is a torus. Mechanics has worked on tori since
      Liouville. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The Möbius chapter supplies the half-twist &mdash; a
      Z&#8322; object, carefully distinguished from the Z&#8323; centre of SU(3) that gives quark charge
      its thirds &mdash; and stakes the framework's one falsifiable prediction on galaxy handedness.</p>

    <h3 id="t-sum-ledger"><a href="/cosmic-ledger/">The Cosmic Ledger</a></h3>
    <p>Polarity is residue: what a collapse discards leaves a lean, and the universe carries the running
      total. The χ-field model assigns χ&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;1 to baryonic matter, χ&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;½ to
      dark matter and χ&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;0 to dark energy, and χ proportional to 1/r gives flat rotation
      curves &mdash; which is motivated and explicitly <em>not</em> derived. The chapter also argues that
      the proton and the electron are one thing at two phases, because their charges cancel to one part in
      ten to the twenty-first and physics admits it has no explanation for that unless the two are related
      by a symmetry. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>

    <h3 id="t-sum-seams"><a href="/seams/">The Seams</a> and <a href="/stress-tests/">The Stress Tests</a></h3>
    <p>A seam is where the interior of a thing and its boundary carry the same information &mdash; black
      hole entropy going as area, the cell membrane, the passing moment, the half-twist &mdash; whose
      mathematical form is Stokes' theorem. The stress tests are the site arguing against itself: the
      entropy objection, the Basel rescue, the base-ten problem, the microtubule evidence given fairly in
      both directions, and the handedness test with its failure conditions written down in advance.</p>
  </div>
</section>

